<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist.Tests</RootNamespace>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.1" />

    <!--
      Two ways of taking the same reading, and they are not interchangeable.

      The collector produces a report and nothing else. It is asked for by name, by
      the collect switch on the test command, so a run that does not ask pays nothing.
      That is the route for reading coverage while working.

      The msbuild integration is the route the floor runs on. It refuses the run when
      the reading falls below Threshold below, which is what makes the floor a rule
      rather than a number in a document, and coverlet is where the comparison lives
      so that nothing in this tree parses a report to make it.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.msbuild" Version="6.0.4">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The floor. It is 100 because that is what the first full measurement over the
    included code was, not because a round number is satisfying. That measurement,
    with the command that produced it, is on issue #50. It is not pasted here: an XML
    comment may not carry two dashes in a row, and a command written without its
    switches is not the command that was run.

    A floor below a measured 100 would admit uncovered code that nobody decided to
    admit, which is the thing this exists to stop. The cost is that the next line
    somebody cannot reach from a test is a decision rather than a slip: either it gets
    a test, or it goes in the exclusion list below with its reason. That is the point,
    and it will be argued again the first time an adapter over a server interface
    lands.

    Line and branch. Method rate is left out because a method with one uncovered line
    already fails on the line rate, and a second number saying the same thing makes a
    failure harder to read rather than easier.

    None of this runs unless CollectCoverage is asked for, so an ordinary test run
    costs nothing and reds on nothing.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Threshold>100</Threshold>
    <ThresholdType>line,branch</ThresholdType>
    <CoverletOutputFormat>cobertura</CoverletOutputFormat>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    What the floor does not cover, one entry with its reason. The list is meant to
    stay short.

    Plugin.cs was on this list and is not any more, which is #34. The reason it carried
    was that reaching a line of it means constructing the server's BasePlugin, which
    takes the server's application paths and its XML serialiser and writes a
    configuration file where the server keeps them, so it could not be exercised
    without a server. The last clause is what was wrong: the base writes wherever
    IApplicationPaths points and parses with whatever IXmlSerializer is handed it, and
    both of those are interfaces. ServerPathsInATemporaryDirectory answers the first
    out of a directory the test owns, PluginConfigurationFile answers the second over
    the framework serialiser, and PluginConfigurationWiringTests constructs the plugin
    on top of the pair. So the save refusal and the load repair are executed rather
    than argued, which is what #34 recorded as proven by nothing.
  -->
  <!--
    What is left, one entry with its reason and with what would retire it. Both
    entries here were added on #25 and both were re-read on #221.

    PluginServiceRegistrator.cs was on this list and is not any more, which is #221.
    The reason it carried was that it is the server's own registration hook, called
    with the server's service collection and its application host, so running it meant
    having both. Neither half held. IServiceCollection is satisfied by ServiceCollection,
    which this project already references, and the application host parameter is never
    read in the body, so nothing stands in for it. The two factories are the part a
    call alone does not reach, because registering a lambda does not run it; they read
    Plugin.Instance, which is constructible from a test since #34, so
    PluginServiceRegistratorTests builds the provider and resolves both.

    LibraryItemDescriber.cs stays, and NEITHER OF THE TWO REASONS IT CARRIED IS THE
    ONE THAT HOLDS. Both are withdrawn here and replaced by one that was measured.

    The first said that reaching a line of it means constructing the server's library
    manager and its user manager, which is a server. That is the same mistake #221
    found on the registrator: ILibraryManager and IUserManager are interfaces. They
    are wide - 84 and 22 member declarations at v10.11.11 - so a hand-written pair is
    a cost, but width is not unreachability and the entry did not claim width.

    The second said the file holds three lookups and a projection with no branch of
    its own that is not a null check. KindOf is a four-arm switch over the item type,
    which is a branch and is not a null check, so that sentence is wrong as written.

    WHAT DOES HOLD is one call, and it is not reached through either parameter. The
    visibility arm calls IsVisible on the BaseItem the library returned, and that path
    reads a process-wide static the server assigns at start-up rather than anything
    handed in: IsVisible goes to IsParentalAllowed, which goes to IsVisibleViaTags,
    which calls GetInheritedTags, whose body calls LibraryManager.GetCollectionFolders
    on the STATIC BaseItem.LibraryManager. Unassigned, that is a null reference before
    any rule of this plugin runs, and the same path reads the static BaseItem.Logger
    on an item with no rating. Faking the two constructor interfaces does not reach
    either static.

    WHAT RETIRES THIS ENTRY: a test that assigns those statics for the length of one
    call and hands the describer a BaseItem and a User, or a seam in this plugin that
    puts the visibility question behind an interface of its own. The statics are
    process-wide, so the first route needs the serialisation PluginInstanceCollection
    already carries for Plugin.Instance. Either way the entry comes off with the test
    that reaches the file rather than with an argument.
  -->
  <!--
    ServerPlaylistGateway.cs is the second entry, and the comment above predicted it
    by name: the argument would be had again the first time an adapter over a server
    interface landed. It is the adapter on issue #82, and this is that argument.

    IPlaylistManager is an interface with thirteen members, so width is not the reason
    and this entry does not claim it. What holds is one call, and it is the same shape
    as the one that keeps the describer here: EntriesOf reads the rows off the Playlist
    the manager hands back, Playlist.GetManageableItems goes to
    Folder.GetLinkedChildrenInfos, and that calls BaseItem.GetLinkedChild, whose first
    branch reads the PROCESS-WIDE STATIC BaseItem.LibraryManager. Unassigned that is a
    null reference before any line of this plugin runs, and faking the constructor
    parameter reaches none of it, because the static is not one.

    THE OTHER FIVE MEMBERS ARE REACHABLE AND THIS ENTRY COVERS THEM ANYWAY. Behind a
    hand-written manager they would run, and what a run of them proves is that a
    forwarder forwards: each is one call with its arguments rearranged and no rule of
    this plugin's own inside it. That is a decision rather than an impossibility, and
    it is taken so the floor is not met by a test asserting that a mock was called.

    WHAT RETIRES THIS ENTRY: a fake manager together with those statics assigned for
    the length of one call, which needs the serialisation PluginInstanceCollection
    already carries for Plugin.Instance; or a seam inside this plugin putting the read
    of a playlist's rows behind an interface of its own.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ExcludeByFile>$(ExcludeByFile)**/LibraryItemDescriber.cs,**/ServerPlaylistGateway.cs</ExcludeByFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The suite constructs the controller and hands it a request context, so it needs the
    same framework the controller compiles against. It is a framework reference rather
    than a package: the assemblies come from the runtime the tests already run on, so
    nothing is added to the dependency graph and no lock file moves.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The plugin project excludes the runtime assets of these two packages, because a
    plugin is loaded next to a server that already carries them. A test process has
    no server around it, so the same assemblies are referenced here with their
    runtime assets kept, and the versions have to match the plugin's by hand until
    one place holds them.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Jellyfin.Controller" Version="10.11.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="Jellyfin.Model" Version="10.11.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    HEADLESS.md is compiled into the test assembly so a test can read it. Issues on
    this board point at that file instead of repeating the rule in it, and an
    embedded copy means the file cannot be deleted or renamed without a red suite.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="HEADLESS.md" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="HeadlessRules.txt" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="HEADLESS-EXCEPTIONS.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The mutation instrument's two files: the scope the run is given, and the register
    of what survived it with a verdict each. Embedded for the reason HEADLESS.md is -
    a document that carries the verdicts is worth as much as the run, and nothing else
    in this tree would notice either of them being deleted or renamed. MutationInstrumentTests
    reads both.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="stryker-config.json" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="MUTATION.md" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The invariant guard's table and register, in the same three-field shape as the
    headless pair above and read by the same scanner. They are data rather than
    source for the same reason: a .cs file holding these literals would be matched
    by a scan reading it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Invariants.txt" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="INVARIANT-EXCEPTIONS.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The visibility gate's register, in the same shape as the pairs above: one line per
    plugin source that takes the entries off a stored document, saying whether it hands
    every read to the gate or reads the document for something other than showing a
    list. Its own header carries where the scan stops. Embedded for the reason the
    others are - nothing else in this tree would notice it being deleted or renamed,
    and VisibilityGateTests reads it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="VISIBILITY-GATE-READERS.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The plugin's own sources, for the invariant guard to read. Same wildcard
    treatment as the suite's sources below, and under a prefix of their own so the
    two scans cannot read each other's files: the headless table judges test code
    and this set is not test code.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist\**\*.cs" Exclude="..\Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist\bin\**;..\Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist\obj\**">
      <LogicalName>pluginsource/%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</LogicalName>
    </EmbeddedResource>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The headless guard reads the suite's own sources. They are embedded by a
    wildcard rather than listed, so a file added tomorrow is scanned the moment it
    is added and nobody has to remember a list. The fixtures the guard is proven
    against are .txt on purpose: they hold the calls the guard refuses, so they
    must not be compiled and must not be scanned as sources.

    A fixture file name carries one extension and no more. A second extension is
    read as a culture by the build, so a file called Something.cs.txt goes into a
    Czech satellite assembly and is missing from the manifest at run time.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="**\*.cs" Exclude="bin\**;obj\**">
      <LogicalName>testsource/%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</LogicalName>
    </EmbeddedResource>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Fixtures\*.json">
      <LogicalName>fixture/%(Filename)%(Extension)</LogicalName>
    </EmbeddedResource>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Fixtures\*.txt">
      <LogicalName>fixture/%(Filename)%(Extension)</LogicalName>
    </EmbeddedResource>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    build.yaml is embedded into the test assembly rather than located on disk at run
    time, so the identity test reads this tree's manifest and never a copy that
    happens to sit beside the test host. The build refreshes it on every run.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\build.yaml" LogicalName="build.yaml" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\CHANGELOG.md" LogicalName="CHANGELOG.md" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\docs\settings.md" LogicalName="settings.md" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\docs\api.md" LogicalName="api.md" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The export sample is embedded from docs/samples for the same reason. It is a file
    this repository offers to somebody writing a reader, and the suite writes it and
    compares it, so it has to be the committed file that is compared and not a copy
    that happens to sit beside the test host.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\docs\samples\watchlist-export.sample.json" LogicalName="watchlist-export.sample.json" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Both project files and both lock files are embedded so a test can compare each pair.
    They are embedded rather than found on disk at run time for the reason build.yaml
    above is: what the test judges has to be this tree's file and never a copy that
    happens to sit beside the test host.

    A missing lock file is refused before this, by the target in Directory.Build.props,
    so these entries are not what makes a deletion loud. Naming the four files one by
    one rather than matching them with a wildcard is still deliberate: a wildcard that
    matched nothing would embed nothing and the tests would fail on a missing resource,
    which is a worse message than a build error naming the file.

    The extension on the embedded lock files is dropped from the logical name. Two
    resources ending in .json would otherwise sit beside the fixtures under a different
    prefix, and the names are what a failing test prints.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist\Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist.csproj" LogicalName="project/Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist.csproj" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist\packages.lock.json" LogicalName="lock/Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist.json" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist.Tests.csproj" LogicalName="project/Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist.Tests.csproj" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="packages.lock.json" LogicalName="lock/Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist.Tests.json" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The document-paste check's own two files, in the same shape as the two pairs
    above: a document a reader meets and a register of declared departures.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="DOCUMENT-PASTES.md" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="DOCUMENT-PASTE-EXCEPTIONS.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The rest of the tree the pasted commands in this repository's documents read,
    under a prefix of its own so nothing else scans it and it scans nothing else.

    ONLY THE PART NOT ALREADY EMBEDDED IS HERE, and that is a constraint of the
    build rather than a choice. A file may be an EmbeddedResource once: including
    it twice under two logical names is NETSDK1022 and stops the build. Everything
    the item groups above already carry - both projects' sources, the fixtures, the
    manifest, the changelog, two documents, both project files and both lock files -
    is put in the tree by DocumentPasteTests instead, which maps those resource
    names onto the repository paths they came from.

    The set is DECLARED rather than being the repository. A command naming a path
    outside it is reported as unjudged rather than answered from a shorter tree,
    which is the failure this set exists against: an answer computed over fewer
    files than the command was given looks exactly like an agreement.

    docs/ is matched by wildcard so a page added tomorrow is in the population the
    day it is added. The root files are named one by one because a wildcard there
    would collide with the changelog above.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\README.md;..\CONTRIBUTING.md;..\NOTICE.md;..\SECURITY.md;..\Directory.Build.props;..\jellyfin.ruleset;..\DCO;..\LICENSE">
      <LogicalName>tree/%(Filename)%(Extension)</LogicalName>
    </EmbeddedResource>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\docs\**\*.md;..\docs\**\*.json" Exclude="..\docs\settings.md;..\docs\api.md;..\docs\samples\watchlist-export.sample.json">
      <LogicalName>tree/docs/%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</LogicalName>
    </EmbeddedResource>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\.github\**\*.yaml;..\.github\**\*.yml;..\.github\**\*.json">
      <LogicalName>tree/.github/%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</LogicalName>
    </EmbeddedResource>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist\**\*.html" Exclude="..\Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist\bin\**;..\Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist\obj\**">
      <LogicalName>tree/Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist/%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</LogicalName>
    </EmbeddedResource>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist\Jellyfin.Plugin.Watchlist.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
